   "text": {
    "L1": "Rust on iron is not dirt stuck to the surface. Rust is a new substance called iron oxide.",
    "L2": "Iron rusts when it reacts with oxygen from the air and water, or moisture.",
    "L3": "Both are needed. Iron in dry air, or in water with no air, hardly rusts.",
    "L4": "The reddish-brown rust is flaky, so it falls away and exposes fresh iron underneath.",
    "L5": "That is why the iron is slowly eaten away and becomes weak.",
    "L6": "Rusting is faster in humid coastal air and during the monsoon, especially when salt is present.",
    "L7": "Paint, oil, grease, zinc or chromium coatings keep air and water away from iron.",
    "L8": "Now choose the two surroundings that iron needs in order to rust."
   },
